+
+  <a name="x-consent"></a>
+  <h2>X-Consent Header</h2>
+  <p>
+The <code>X-Consent</code> HTTP request header MAY be used on FHIR RESTful interactions
+to indicate that the client is asserting that it holds, or has collected, consent
+material that authorizes the request. The header value is a URI that identifies where
+the server can retrieve or inspect the asserted consent material. That URI might
+identify a FHIR <a href="consent.html">Consent</a> resource, a FHIR <a href="binary.html">Binary</a>
+resource containing a scanned consent form, or another retrievable document such as
+a PDF consent form.
+  </p>
+  <p>
+The client is responsible for sending <code>X-Consent</code> only when it believes the
+referenced consent material is applicable to the request and can be made available
+to the server. The server remains responsible for determining whether the response
+is authorized under the server's policy. A server MAY ignore the header, but SHOULD
+process it when it supports this mechanism. A server MAY retrieve the URI to support
+its authorization decision, and MAY reject the request if required consent material
+cannot be retrieved or otherwise verified.
+  </p>
+  <p>
+The header can be used with any RESTful interaction, though it is most useful for
+queries and retrieves where a client presents point-of-care consent that the server
+would not otherwise have on file. If retrieving or verifying the referenced material
+will take time, the server can use the <a href="async.html">asynchronous request pattern</a>.
+Servers that cache retrieved consent material to reduce round trips SHOULD keep such
+caches short lived or validate them using standard HTTP mechanisms such as entity
+tags, and SHALL ensure that cache use remains consistent with consent revocation
+requirements. This specification does not define how a client discovers that a
+server supports <code>X-Consent</code>, nor how support is expressed in a CapabilityStatement.
+  </p>
